Impetigo
Common Infections and Afflictions
Honey-colored crusts, Erythematous base, Highly contagious localized spread

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-018
Reason for Visit: Rapidly spreading, weeping facial sores.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting an explosive onset of rapidly spreading, weeping, and crusted lesions across the face over the past 24 hours. The highly contagious nature of the described lesions, combined with rapid localized spread, necessitates an urgent on-site evaluation to definitively differentiate from severe viral presentations (e.g., eczema herpeticum or disseminated herpes simplex) and to immediately institute targeted antibiotic therapy to arrest the infectious spread. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable public health risk of wider transmission and severe localized tissue breakdown, fulfilling the criteria for MBS urgent after-hours item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: These crusty yellow sores appeared out of nowhere yesterday, and they are spreading across my face so incredibly fast.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 48 hours ago as minor, superficial vesicles around the anterior nares following mild mechanical trauma (nose-blowing during a minor viral upper respiratory tract infection). Over the past 24 hours, the patient reports these vesicles ruptured easily, evolving into classic honey-colored crusted lesions. The infection exhibits a highly contagious nature with rapid localized spread to the perioral region and unilateral cheek. Patient notes mild pruritus around the affected area but stresses the painless nature of the lesions themselves. Denies systemic toxicity, high fever, or oral mucosal involvement.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living with family. Extremely high risk of intra-household transmission due to the highly contagious exudate.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 120/78 mmHg | HR: 80 bpm | Temp: 37.2 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Alert, visibly distressed by the facial disfigurement. Frequent attempts to scratch the lesions are noted.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. A bacterial swab culture isolating Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes would assist in confirming the diagnosis, but empiric treatment must not be delayed.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination of the face reveals multiple coalescing, superficial vesicles and pustules primarily distributed around the nares, upper lip, and right cheek. The older lesions have ruptured, leaving thick, adherent, honey-colored crusts overlying a bright erythematous base. No deep ulceration or necrotic tissue is present. The lesions are non-tender to light palpation. Mild, slightly tender right anterior cervical and submandibular regional lymphadenopathy is present. Oral mucosa is clear.
Environmental Observations: Shared household items (towels, linens) are present, increasing transmission risk.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has applied an over-the-counter hydrocortisone cream, which profoundly suppresses local immunity and rapidly accelerates the bacterial spread.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Impetigo - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Herpes Simplex Virus (Cold Sores) - Ruled out clinically by the lack of classic grouped vesicles on a singular erythematous base, the presence of thick honey-colored crusts (rather than serous scabbing), and the rapid contiguous spread beyond the vermilion border.
Eczema Herpeticum - Unlikely given the lack of a severe underlying atopic dermatitis flare, the absence of punched-out monomorphic viral ulcers, and the lack of systemic toxicity.
Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out due to the presence of frank purulence/honey-crusting and localized lymphadenopathy, indicating a primary infectious process rather than an allergic one.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (High intra-household transmission risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The explosive spread of weeping facial lesions mandated an urgent home visit to rapidly exclude aggressive or vision-threatening dermatological infections, particularly given the patient's inappropriate use of topical corticosteroids. Clinical examination confirmed non-bullous impetigo. Immediate intervention with potent systemic antibiotics and the institution of strict, targeted infection control protocols was critical to arrest the rapid physical spread and prevent immediate household transmission. This timely diagnosis and containment definitively could not be safely delayed until regular consulting hours, satisfying the urgent criteria of MBS items 585-600.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Flucloxacillin 500mg strictly four times daily for 7 days due to the extensive facial spread and multiple lesions (making topical therapy alone insufficient). Instructed immediate cessation of all topical corticosteroid use.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically remove the infected crusts and allow the oral antibiotics to reach the underlying tissue efficiently, soak a clean cloth in warm, soapy water and gently press it against the sores for 5 minutes twice a day to soften the scabs before carefully wiping them away. Leaving the thick crusts intact creates a physical shield that protects the actively multiplying bacteria from your immune system.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 48-72 hours to ensure the lesions are drying and no new lesions are forming.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the Emergency Department immediately if the redness becomes deep, hot, and severely painful, if you develop a high fever with uncontrollable shivering, or if the eye becomes swollen shut.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; swab results (if taken) take 48-72 hours. Therapy covers the most common bacterial pathogens.
Clinical Course: Lesions should cease spreading within 48 hours of commencing oral antibiotics. Complete healing without scarring is expected in 7 to 10 days, provided the crusts are not deeply excoriated.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate review if your urine turns dark brown or cola-colored within the next 2-3 weeks, as this can be a rare kidney complication linked to certain strains of this skin infection.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: This is a highly contagious, superficial bacterial infection (often 'Staph' or 'Strep'). The oral antibiotics will circulate through your bloodstream to kill the bacteria from the inside out.
Actionable Steps: You are strictly considered contagious until you have completed 24 hours of targeted antibiotic therapy. You must wash all your bed sheets, pillowcases, and towels immediately in hot water. Do not share any personal items with household members, and wash your hands rigorously with antibacterial soap after performing wound care.

